{title2=4289}
{title2=CP2}

Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/physics_preliminary_examinations/response/1162943/attach/3/Twain.7z?cookie_passthrough=1[Oxford source archive], member `2013/cp2_june_2013_final.pdf`. The cover dates this ordinary sitting to Tuesday 4 June 2013. All eleven questions are treated. Unlettered marked blocks use ordinal subparts; printed Roman subparts are retained.

== 1
{scope}

=== Solution

<Newton's second law> applies separately to each body's total force: zero force on $q_1$ means zero acceleration of $q_1$, while a nonzero force on a free $q_2$ gives nonzero acceleration of $q_2$. It does not assert that those two forces must be equal and opposite.

The conducting shell is a third interacting body. Its induced surface charges shield the cavity from the external charge, while charges on its outer surface can still exert a force on $q_2$. Reaction forces act on the shell and, if it is held fixed, on its supports. In the electrostatic limit the force balance of the complete isolated charge–shell system is $\mathbf F_1+\mathbf F_2+\mathbf F_{\rm shell}=0$. Thus the given situation is consistent with both <Newton's second law> and <Newton's third law> once the shell is included.

Shielding removes the cavity field due to external charges; it does not generally remove the force of the inner surface charges induced by an off-centre $q_1$. Zero total force on $q_1$ is an additional condition, satisfied for example when it is at the centre of a spherical cavity.

== 2
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== Solution

The <electric field> is force per unit positive stationary test charge, in the limit that the test charge does not disturb the source:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E.}
$$
The <magnetic flux density> is defined by the velocity-dependent part of the <Lorentz force>, $\mathbf F_B=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$. Equivalently, a straight current element in a uniform field experiences
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F=I\boldsymbol\ell\times\mathbf B,}
$$
which fixes its magnitude and right-hand-rule direction by force measurements with different current orientations.

=== 2
{scope}

==== Solution

For a <point charge> $q_j$, <Coulomb's law> gives $\mathbf E_j=q_j\hat{\mathbf r}/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$. The flux through an oriented surface element is
$$
\mathbf E_j\cdot d\mathbf S=\frac{q_j}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\frac{\hat{\mathbf r}\cdot d\mathbf S}{r^2}=\frac{q_j}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\,d\Omega.
$$
Here $d\Omega$ is its signed <solid angle> seen from the charge. A closed surface subtends total oriented solid angle $4\pi$ for a point inside and zero for a point outside. Thus the flux of each inside charge is $q_j/\epsilon_0$ and each outside charge has zero net flux. By <superposition>,
$$
\boxed{\oint_S\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf S=\frac1{\epsilon_0}\sum_{j\text{ inside}}q_j=\frac{q_{\rm enclosed}}{\epsilon_0}.}
$$
This is <Gauss's law>; a surface passing through a point-charge singularity is excluded.

== 3
{scope}

=== Solution

In classical <Maxwell's equations>, electric charge is a source or sink of the <electric field>, so in vacuum $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\epsilon_0$ can be nonzero. There is no magnetic-charge source in the same theory: <Gauss's law for magnetism> is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$. Magnetic flux entering a closed surface equals the flux leaving it. A magnetic dipole has both polarities; it is not an isolated magnetic monopole.

== 4
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $J_1,J_2$ be the single-slit intensities at the two wavelengths. At a screen position $y$, the path difference is approximately $\Delta=d\sin\theta$, with $\sin\theta=y/\sqrt{D^2+y^2}$. Fields from the two slits interfere at each wavelength, while a detector averaging over the optical beat period adds the intensities of the two distinct wavelengths:
$$
\boxed{I(y)=4J_1\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi\Delta}{\lambda_1}\right)+4J_2\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi\Delta}{\lambda_2}\right).}
$$
For equal single-slit intensities $J_1=J_2=J$, define $\bar\sigma=(1/\lambda_1+1/\lambda_2)/2$ and $\Delta\sigma=1/\lambda_2-1/\lambda_1$. Then
$$
I=4J\left[1+\cos(\pi\Delta\sigma\Delta)\cos(2\pi\bar\sigma\Delta)\right].
$$
The first cosine gives the <interference visibility> envelope. Near the centre one may further replace $\Delta$ by $dy/D$. The formula neglects unspecified single-slit envelopes and the slow change of single-slit intensity across the screen.

=== 2
{scope}

==== Solution

For the centred screen, $|y|\le0.30$ m. Retaining the screen-angle geometry gives
$$
\Delta_{\max}=\frac{d(0.30)}{\sqrt{0.50^2+0.30^2}}=5.145\,\mu\mathrm m,\qquad
\bar\lambda=\frac1{\bar\sigma}=512.20\,\mathrm{nm}.
$$
There are about $\bar\sigma\Delta_{\max}=10.045$ carrier orders on each side. With equal spectral intensities, direct extrema of the intensity formula give
$$
\boxed{21\text{ bright fringes: the central maximum and ten on each side}.}
$$
Their contrast declines toward the edges: the first equal-intensity visibility zero occurs at $\Delta=1/(2\Delta\sigma)=5.25\,\mu\mathrm m$, just beyond the screen's $5.145\,\mu\mathrm m$ limit. Edge visibility is only about $0.031$, so fewer fringes may be discernible at a practical contrast threshold.

The screen reaches $|y|/D=0.6$, where a paraxial conversion of its full width is inaccurate. Also, wavelength-independent detector sensitivity does not itself establish equal source intensities. The boxed count specifies the usual equal-intensity idealization; without spectral weights and a visibility threshold, a unique experimental count is not fixed.

\Image[2013-two-colour-fringes.png]
{title=Two-colour double-slit fringes across the stated 60 cm screen}
{description=Equal intensities at 500 and 525 nm are added incoherently between colours. The actual screen-angle geometry gives 21 local maxima, with low contrast near the edges.}

== 5
{scope}

=== Solution

Take B as reference and define positive source current $I$ upward into the upper internal node; the source symbol does not specify an arrow, so this definition fixes its sign. With A open, no current flows through the upper series resistor. The shunt resistor therefore has voltage $IR$, and the battery's longer plate is on the right, adding $+V$ toward A. Thus
$$
\boxed{V_{\rm Th}=V_A-V_B=IR+V.}
$$
Deactivate the independent sources: open the ideal current source and short the ideal voltage source. The two resistors then lie in series between A and B, giving
$$
\boxed{R_{\rm Th}=2R.}
$$
The <Thévenin equivalent> is a source $IR+V$ in series with $2R$, positive toward A. If $I$ is instead defined downward, replace $I$ by $-I$; the resistance is unchanged.

== 6
{scope}

=== Solution

The ideal voltage source drives a series $L$–$R$–$C$ branch and a separate resistor $R$ in parallel. At $\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}$, the series branch is purely resistive, so its peak current is $I_p=V_0/R$. Its stored energies are
$$
U_L=\frac12LI_p^2\sin^2\omega_0t,\qquad U_C=\frac12LI_p^2\cos^2\omega_0t.
$$
Their sum is constant in sinusoidal steady state, with value $U_{\max}=LV_0^2/(2R^2)$. Each resistor dissipates average power $V_0^2/(2R)$, including the resistor directly across the source. Over one cycle,
$$
W_{\rm diss}=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_0}\frac{V_0^2}{R},\qquad
\boxed{\frac{U_{\max}}{W_{\rm diss}}=\frac{\omega_0L}{4\pi R}=\frac1{4\pi R}\sqrt{\frac LC}.}
$$
Omitting the parallel resistor would double this ratio. The expression concerns the driven steady state, not a switch-on transient.

== 7
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== Solution

The upper positive plate gives a downward field $E=V/a$, while $\mathbf B$ points into the page. For a rightward electron, the electric force is upward and the magnetic force downward. Its initial speed is
$$
v_0=\sqrt{\frac{2(1\,\mathrm{eV})}{m_e}}=5.931\times10^5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\frac EB=\frac{20000}{0.0337(1)}=5.935\times10^5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.
$$
These are equal to the precision intended by the rounded plate spacing: the arrangement is a <velocity selector>. Accordingly,
$$
\boxed{\theta_{\rm exit}\simeq0.}
$$
It is not appropriate to calculate a large electric deflection and ignore magnetic bending. For completeness, if all printed values are treated literally, let $v_d=E/B$, $u=v_0-v_d$, and $\omega_c=eB/m_e$. The exact nonrelativistic crossed-field motion is
$$
x=v_dt+\frac{u}{\omega_c}\sin\omega_ct,\qquad y=\frac{u}{\omega_c}(\cos\omega_ct-1),
$$
with $v_x=v_d+u\cos\omega_ct$ and $v_y=-u\sin\omega_ct$. At the exit solve $x(t_e)=0.50$ m, then use $\theta=\operatorname{atan2}(v_y,v_x)$. The small rounding mismatch produces nanometre-scale oscillations, with $|\theta|\le\sin^{-1}(|u|/v_d)\simeq6.32\times10^{-4}$ rad. Its precise oscillatory exit angle is not robust to the supplied rounding.

=== 2
{scope}

==== Solution

In uniform fields, a rigid neutral <electric dipole> with both charges moving at the selected common velocity has zero net translational force. To leading nonrelativistic order its torque is $\boldsymbol\tau=\mathbf p\times(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$, which also vanishes at the velocity-selector condition. Thus it continues undeflected and, initially nonrotating, does not start rotating in the ideal balanced fields. An unbalanced effective field could exert a torque; the dipole orientation is not otherwise specified.

== 8
{scope}

=== i
{scope}

==== Solution

Ground the non-inverting input and take the ideal <operational amplifier> in stable, unsaturated <negative feedback>. Its inverting input is a <virtual ground> and draws no current. <Kirchhoff's current law> there gives
$$
\frac{V_i}{R_i}+\frac{V_o}{R_f}=0,\qquad
\boxed{\frac{V_o}{V_i}=-\frac{R_f}{R_i},\quad |A_v|=\frac{R_f}{R_i}.}
$$
The minus sign is the phase inversion of the <inverting amplifier>.

=== ii
{scope}

==== Solution

The input source supplies $I_i=V_i/R_i$ into the <virtual ground>, so the complete circuit's input resistance is $R_i$. The ideal output is a zero-resistance controlled voltage source:
$$
\boxed{R_{\rm in}=R_i,\qquad R_{\rm out}=0.}
$$
These are properties of the closed-loop circuit, distinct from the infinite input resistance of the amplifier terminals themselves.

=== iii
{scope}

==== Solution

With $R_f=R_i$, the output and input voltage magnitudes are equal. The signal power supplied at the input is $P_i=V_{i,\rm rms}^2/R_i$, while the load receives $P_L=V_{o,\rm rms}^2/R_L$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{P_L}{P_i}=\left(\frac{R_f}{R_i}\right)^2\frac{R_i}{R_L}=\frac{1000}{100}=10.}
$$
This is $10$ dB of power gain into the specified load. Output energy comes from the amplifier's supply rails; unity voltage-gain magnitude does not imply unity power gain. Dissipation in the feedback resistor is additional to the specified load power.

=== iv
{scope}

==== Solution

Replace the feedback resistor by a capacitor $C_f$, retaining the input resistor $R_i$. The <virtual ground> makes the input charge flow onto the feedback capacitor rather than into the ideal amplifier. Integrating its current gives
$$
V_o(t)-V_o(0)=-\frac1{C_f}\int_0^t I_i(t')\,dt',\qquad
\boxed{\frac{\Delta V_o}{Q_{\rm in}}=-\frac1{C_f}.}
$$
If the calorimeter delivers $Q_{\rm in}=\kappa\mathcal E$ for deposited energy $\mathcal E$, then $\Delta V_o=-\kappa\mathcal E/C_f$. A reset establishes the initial output; a practical large resistor across $C_f$ limits long-time drift, but is omitted from the ideal integration interval.

\Image[2013-charge-integrator.png]
{title=Ideal inverting charge integrator for the calorimeter signal}
{description=The original feedback resistor is replaced by C_f; the input resistor remains for the subsequent voltage-driven integrator calculation.}

=== v
{scope}

==== Solution

For the modified circuit, $I_i=V_i/R_i$ and $\dot V_o=-V_i/(R_iC_f)$. With $V_i=V_0\cos\omega t$,
$$
\boxed{V_o(t)=V_o(0)-\frac{V_0}{\omega R_iC_f}\sin\omega t.}
$$
The sinusoidal component has amplitude $V_0/(\omega R_iC_f)$ and leads the cosine input by $90^\circ$; the constant is set by the initial capacitor charge. This assumes the output remains within the amplifier's linear range.

== 9
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== Solution

<Fermat's principle> says that the travel time, or equivalently the <optical path length>, is stationary under small path variations at fixed endpoints. For a flat interface, let the ray cross at horizontal coordinate $x$, with source and detector at heights $h_1,h_2$ and horizontal separation $D$. Apart from $1/c$, its travel time is
$$
\mathcal L(x)=n_1\sqrt{h_1^2+x^2}+n_2\sqrt{h_2^2+(D-x)^2}.
$$
Setting $d\mathcal L/dx=0$ gives
$$
\frac{n_1x}{\sqrt{h_1^2+x^2}}=\frac{n_2(D-x)}{\sqrt{h_2^2+(D-x)^2}},\qquad
\boxed{n_1\sin i=n_2\sin r.}
$$
The angles are measured to the normal. This is <Snell's law>; “least time” is more generally a stationarity condition.

=== i
{scope}

==== Solution

Take a horizontal cross-section in units of $R$. The eye is at $E=(\sqrt2,0)$, and the tadpole swims along the perpendicular diameter $T=(0,y)$ shown in the source. Since glass and water have the same red-light index, their internal interface causes no red-light refraction. Write an exit point on the circular wall as $P=(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)$. An unobstructed outward ray to the eye has $|\phi|\le\pi/4$.

For $\phi\ge0$, let $e$ be the magnitude of the emerging ray's angle to the outward normal and $i$ its internal angle. Geometry and <Snell's law> give
$$
\sin e=\frac{\sqrt2\sin\phi}{\sqrt{3-2\sqrt2\cos\phi}},\qquad
i=\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{\sin e}{1.33}\right).
$$
The outgoing internal ray makes angle $\beta=\phi-i$ to $+x$. Its backward continuation intersects the swim diameter at
$$
y(\phi)=\sin\phi-\cos\phi\tan(\phi-i).
$$
The directly visible positive endpoint is the largest such intercept, including all single-pass refracted rays. Maximizing this one-variable expression gives $\phi_*\simeq38.07^\circ$ and $y_*\simeq0.75744$. By symmetry the visible length is $2Ry_*$, so
$$
\boxed{\text{visible fraction}\simeq0.757\quad\text{(about three quarters)}.}
$$
Checking only the grazing exit at $\phi=45^\circ$ gives $\sqrt2/[1+\sqrt{1.33^2-1}]\simeq0.75350$, a close estimate but a slightly smaller fraction. The exact limiting ray occurs before that grazing ray, where two refracted paths merge at a <caustic>. This is a geometrical, single-pass visibility boundary; finite pupil size and detection thresholds would smooth the transition.

\Image[2013-tank-visible-diameter.png]
{title=Single-pass visibility across the fish-tank diameter}
{description=The original ray construction traces rays backward from the eye through a circular n=1.33 interface. The maximum swim-line intercept, not just the grazing outgoing ray, determines the visible interval.}

=== ii
{scope}

==== Solution

The result need not be the same. <Optical dispersion> changes the <refractive index> with wavelength. Under <normal dispersion>, the blue-light index is larger, changing the refracted rays and generally reducing the visible fraction in the one-interface model. The blue indices of both water and glass are not specified; they may also differ from each other, introducing refraction at their interface. Thus no unique numerical blue-light fraction follows from the red-light value alone.

=== iii
{scope}

==== Solution

For near-normal viewing through the flat top surface, the refracted ray appears to originate at an apparent depth $h_{\rm app}$. A ray at horizontal offset $x$ has $x=h\tan\theta_w=h_{\rm app}\tan\theta_a$, and <Snell's law> gives $n\sin\theta_w=\sin\theta_a$. In the paraxial limit,
$$
\boxed{h_{\rm app}=\frac hn=\frac{20\,\mathrm{cm}}{1.33}\simeq15.0\,\mathrm{cm}.}
$$
At an oblique viewing angle the exact ray-based apparent depth is $h\tan\theta_w/\tan\theta_a$; the simple ratio presumes the usual near-normal top view.

== 10
{scope}

=== i
{scope}

==== Solution

Let the wire lie along $z$, and observe at perpendicular distance $r$. In the <Biot-Savart law>, the azimuthal contributions from line elements add with magnitude
$$
B(r)=\frac{\mu_0I_0}{4\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{r\,dz}{(r^2+z^2)^{3/2}}=\frac{\mu_0I_0}{2\pi r}.
$$
Independently, cylindrical symmetry makes the field constant and azimuthal on a circular <Amperian loop>, so <Ampère's law> gives $2\pi rB=\mu_0I_0$. Both yield
$$
\boxed{\mathbf B=\frac{\mu_0I_0}{2\pi r}\hat{\boldsymbol\phi},}
$$
with direction fixed by the right-hand rule.

=== ii
{scope}

==== Solution

For a thin current-carrying cylindrical shell, a coaxial <Amperian loop> encloses zero current inside and the entire current outside. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf B(R^-)=0,\qquad \mathbf B(R^+)=\frac{\mu_0I_0}{2\pi R}\hat{\boldsymbol\phi}.}
$$
More generally $B(r)=0$ for $r<R$ and $B(r)=\mu_0I_0/(2\pi r)$ for $r>R$. The tangential field discontinuity is supported by the surface current.

=== iii
{scope}

==== Solution

Let the column radius be $b=\sqrt{A/\pi}$, and $J=I_0/A$ be its uniform axial <current density>. Treating it as concentric shells, only the current at radii below $r$ contributes to $B(r)$: a surrounding shell has zero interior field. Thus
$$
B_\phi(r)=\frac{\mu_0(J\pi r^2)}{2\pi r}=\frac{\mu_0Jr}{2}.
$$
The magnetic force density is radially inward, $\mathbf J\times\mathbf B=-(\mu_0J^2r/2)\hat{\mathbf r}$. Radial mechanical equilibrium at a fixed height requires
$$
\frac{dp}{dr}=-\frac{\mu_0J^2r}{2},\qquad p(r)-p(b)=\frac{\mu_0J^2}{4}(b^2-r^2).
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{p(0)-p(b)=\frac{\mu_0J^2b^2}{4}=\frac{\mu_0I_0^2}{4\pi A}.}
$$
The pressure is higher at the centre: its outward force balances the inward magnetic pinch. Vertical hydrostatic variation is separate from this radial pressure difference.

== 11
{scope}

=== i
{scope}

==== Solution

Ignoring edge fields, <Gauss's law> gives $E=\sigma/\epsilon_0$ between the parallel plates. Their voltage is $V=Ea=\sigma a/\epsilon_0$, and their charge magnitude is $Q=\sigma s^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{C_0=\frac QV=\frac{\epsilon_0s^2}{a}.}
$$

=== ii
{scope}

==== Solution

Take $x\in[-s/2,s/2]$ along the tilt, with local separation $h(x)=a+2\delta x/s$. Since $s\gg a$ and the tilt is small, each narrow strip is a local <parallel-plate capacitor> at the same voltage $V$. Its surface charge densities are approximately
$$
\boxed{\sigma_\pm(x)=\pm\frac{\epsilon_0V}{a+2\delta x/s}.}
$$
Parallel strip capacitances add, so for $|\delta|<a$,
$$
C(\delta)=\epsilon_0s\int_{-s/2}^{s/2}\frac{dx}{a+2\delta x/s}
=\frac{\epsilon_0s^2}{2\delta}\log\frac{a+\delta}{a-\delta}.
$$
Using $\log[(1+z)/(1-z)]=2(z+z^3/3+z^5/5+\cdots)$ gives
$$
\boxed{C(\delta)=\frac{\epsilon_0s^2}{a}\left[1+\frac{\delta^2}{3a^2}+O\left(\frac{\delta^4}{a^4}\right)\right].}
$$
The denser charge at the smaller gap explains why tilting increases the capacitance. The local-strip approximation neglects fringing and slope corrections of order $(\delta/s)^2$, smaller than the retained $(\delta/a)^2$ term.

=== iii
{scope}

==== Solution

Isolation fixes charge, not voltage: $Q=C_0V_0$. The <electrostatic energy> is therefore $U(t)=Q^2/[2C(\delta(t))]$. Inverting the small-tilt capacitance gives
$$
\boxed{U(t)=\frac{\epsilon_0s^2V_0^2}{2a}\left[1-\frac{\delta_0^2}{3a^2}\sin^2\omega_0t+O\left(\frac{\delta_0^4}{a^4}\right)\right].}
$$
The leading variation has frequency $2\omega_0$, since tilts of opposite sign give the same capacitance. With $U_0=C_0V_0^2/2$, the result is $U_0[1-\delta_0^2/(6a^2)+(\delta_0^2/(6a^2))\cos2\omega_0t]$ to this order. Energy decreases as the gap becomes less uniform at fixed charge; the difference is exchanged mechanically with the oscillator.

\Image[2013-tilted-capacitor.png]
{title=Capacitance increase and fixed-charge energy decrease under a small tilt}
{description=The exact local-strip capacitance is compared with its quadratic expansion. The isolated capacitor has fixed total charge and an energy modulation at twice the mechanical frequency.}
